1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:04,280
Hi everyone, I'm Ashrith Repaka and this is FordCast, a

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:08,320
bilingual trip checker for Central Texas drivers approaching low-water

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:08,800
crossings.

4
00:00:10,133 --> 00:00:12,983
Lake Austin is a part of Texas' Colorado River.

5
00:00:13,683 --> 00:00:17,543
In July 2025, swift water and debris prompted waterway

6
00:00:17,543 --> 00:00:17,923
restrictions.

7
00:00:18,823 --> 00:00:22,163
FordCast helps those drivers check road crossings before leaving.

8
00:00:23,420 --> 00:00:27,380
For example, I'll check downtown Austin to Onion

9
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:28,020
Creek Park.

10
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:32,520
As you can see here, FordCast compares the route with

11
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:36,320
2,052 inventory records and groups hardware records

12
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:37,480
into crossing areas.

13
00:00:38,753 --> 00:00:42,753
Here are official reports, available creek readings, and nearby

14
00:00:42,753 --> 00:00:43,553
flood history.

15
00:00:44,473 --> 00:00:46,753
The summary also identifies missing sensor coverage.

16
00:00:47,533 --> 00:00:49,753
A missing report never proves that a road is dry.

17
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:54,860
These TypeScript checks compare road names,

18
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:57,140
waterway names, and distance.

19
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:01,840
Grouping the flasher arms keeps multiple hardware records from

20
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:03,400
inflating the crossing count.

21
00:01:04,733 --> 00:01:08,033
Now, I'll add a clearly labeled test closure.

22
00:01:08,793 --> 00:01:12,153
FordCast requests another route and checks for remaining hazards.

23
00:01:13,053 --> 00:01:16,233
Test routes cannot be sent to Google Maps until the test ends.

24
00:01:17,620 --> 00:01:21,440
The interface supports both English and Spanish.

25
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:25,660
The drive view offers large repeat, mute, and stop controls.

26
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:28,720
This recording shows the mobile interface in a browser.

27
00:01:30,167 --> 00:01:33,387
This is an archived Memorial Day 2015

28
00:01:33,387 --> 00:01:34,487
simulation.

29
00:01:35,407 --> 00:01:39,267
Historical readings change the crossing's caution state and select a pre

30
00:01:39,267 --> 00:01:40,107
-computed alternative.

31
00:01:40,987 --> 00:01:42,867
The car position is simulated too.

32
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:48,360
The Python replay checks both observation and availability

33
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:48,980
times.

34
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:53,760
It rejects readings that arrive after its clock, keeping future information

35
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:55,320
out of the historical test.

36
00:01:56,487 --> 00:02:00,207
Across 12 downstream gauge reference cases,

37
00:02:00,927 --> 00:02:04,787
the upstream rule detected 10, missed 2, and produced

38
00:02:04,787 --> 00:02:06,027
6 false alarms.

39
00:02:07,067 --> 00:02:10,687
The nearest gauge baseline detected 4 with 3 false alarms,

40
00:02:11,667 --> 00:02:14,027
road-flood prediction remains unvalidated.

41
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:19,460
FordCast uses TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, and Expo.

42
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:23,720
AI assistance from Claude Code and Codex was a factor in creating FordCast.

43
00:02:24,780 --> 00:02:28,800
My direct contribution was organizing the datasets and checking

44
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:32,780
functionality, while Ashrith was creating a UI that's essential for

45
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:34,820
users to find dangerous water crossings.

46
00:02:36,167 --> 00:02:39,427
Next comes road level validation and user testing.

47
00:02:40,227 --> 00:02:43,587
Obey official warnings and barricades and never drive into water.
